<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicaea</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as İznik, Iznik</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicaea, modern İznik in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, was an ancient and Byzantine city on the eastern shore of Lake Ascania. It hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">First Council of Nicaea</a> in 325, which produced the Nicene Creed, and the Second Council in 787. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> held it as their capital from about 1081 until the First Crusade restored it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> in 1097; from 1204 to 1261 it was the seat of the Empire of Nicaea, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it in 1331.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicaea.jpg" alt="Nicaea" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dosseman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iznik_Roman_Theatre_1645.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
